<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oxford is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, home to one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was among the universities where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> was studied. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> note that Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Oxford, became a foundation of European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford.jpg" alt="Oxford" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Hisgett from Birmingham, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Oxford_(5652685943).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The universities of <b>Oxford</b> and Cambridge, which emerged in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries, trained clergy and officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its palm-leaf manuscript has been held in the Bodleian Library at <b>Oxford</b> since 1627.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ulugh Beg's Samarkand star catalogue of over a thousand stars was among the most accurate before the telescope, and was published at <b>Oxford</b> in 1665.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The culture was rediscovered through the tin mines of the colonial era and first studied by Bernard Fagg (1915–1987), who founded the museum at Jos in 1952 and excavated at Taruga before becoming curator of the Pitt Rivers Museum in <b>Oxford</b> in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Oxford</b> Encyclopedia of British Literature sees it over in British and American literature by about 1939, and Clement Greenberg in the 1930s, except in the visual and performing arts; in music, Paul Griffiths notes, a new generation of composers revived it after the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The fasces, the Roman bundle of rods tied round an axe that gave fascism its name, was used before 1914 mostly by left-wing, liberal and republican movements; it appears on the Sheldonian Theater at <b>Oxford</b> and on the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
